<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Maryland</h1><p class="st-meta">1632 CE – 1776 CE · capital Annapolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The proprietary colony of the Calvert family on Chesapeake Bay, founded as a refuge for English Catholics and known for its 1649 Toleration Act.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-calvert-1st-baron-baltimore">George Calvert</a>, first Baron Baltimore, sought a colony where Catholics could live freely, and in 1632 Charles I granted the charter to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-calvert">Cecil Calvert</a>. In 1634 the ships Ark and Dove brought the first settlers, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-calvert">Leonard Calvert</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-marys-city-maryland">St Mary's City</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potomac-river">Potomac</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Calverts held the colony as a proprietary palatinate with wide powers, though an elected assembly met from 1635. Catholics remained a minority, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland-toleration-act">Toleration Act</a> of 1649 protected Trinitarian Christians. Tobacco grown by servants and enslaved Africans was the mainstay. After the Protestant revolt of 1689 the Crown took over, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> was established, and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annapolis-maryland">Annapolis</a> in 1694. The Protestant Calverts regained the province in 1715.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mason-dixon-line">Mason-Dixon line</a>, surveyed from 1763, settled the long border dispute with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-pennsylvania">Province of Pennsylvania</a>. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-eden-governor-of-maryland">Robert Eden</a> left in 1776, and Maryland declared independence as a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-maryland.jpg" alt="Province of Maryland" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Calvert family · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calvert_family_banner.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1632 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Annapolis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cecil Calvert, Leonard Calvert, Robert Eden</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maryland (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1704&amp;focus=province-of-maryland" aria-label="Province of Maryland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 780 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Maryland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1036L4970 663M3105 1036L4997 651M3105 1036L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0M4997 651h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1036h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Annapolis. <a href="/atlas?year=1704&amp;focus=province-of-maryland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
